'''''Peperomia urvilleana''''' is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Piperaceae]], native to the Solomon Islands, New Caledonia, Norfolk Island, the southwestern Pacific<ref name="POWO_680140-1" >{{cite POWO |id=680140-1 |title=''Peperomia urvilleana'' A.Rich. |access-date=17 April 2023 }}</ref> and warmer areas of New Zealand (only [[Marlborough Sounds]] and [[Golden Bay / Mohua]] to the [[Heaphy River|Heaphy]] in South Island).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Peperomia urvilleana |url=https://www.nzpcn.org.nz/flora/species/peperomia-urvilleana/ |access-date=2024-10-30 |website=New Zealand Plant Conservation Network |language=en}}</ref> It is a succulent subshrub of the forest floor and occasionally grows as a low trunk epiphyte.<ref>{{cite journal |title=New Zealand Epiphytes |last1=Oliver |first1=W. R. B. |journal=Journal of Ecology |year=1930 |volume=18 |issue=1 |pages=1–50 |doi=10.2307/2255890 |jstor=2255890 }}</ref> Its [[Māori language|Māori]] name is wharanui.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |title=wharanui |url=https://maoridictionary.co.nz/word/10103 |access-date=2024-10-30 |website=Te Aka Māori Dictionary |language=en}}</ref>
